            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="3" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>



First, let me start off by confirming your house has a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) installed in that small cubby hole behind the fridge. HRV's are usually installed in houses that are "tighter" and require supplemental ventilation to maintain indoor air quality. You will see various "high wall" openings around the house that are air exhausts from the HRV (the ones in the bathrooms I showed you). I'm not exactly sure if the air supplies from this HRV are dedicated openings throughout the house, or if it uses the furnace ductwork to distribute the fresh air. What I am sure of is that the HRV was not working when I visited your home and it doesn't appear to have operated for quite some time.



Based on the amount of natural air leakage &amp; other exhaust ventilation from your home (bathroom fans &amp; clothes dryer), it appears that your house currently does not require the HRV's operation. Please see my section on "Ventilation" in the "Additional Comments" section of this report - including an option for how you may want to use the fresh air supply to that older HRV and have it connected (with a manual damper) to your return air ductwork for the furnace (or new boiler fancoil).



So .......... overall, your house is pretty good for air tightness. There are a few areas that could be addressed:



1. The HRV itself had temperatures as low at 13C being distributed through it to those high wall bathroom vents (I had you feel the cold air coming in). The house temperature was 19C and outside temp was 6C at the time of my evaluation. The interior dampers in the HRV may be in the "open" position, allowing heat loss even though the unit iis not operating.



2. The mechanical bathroom fans also allowed air leakage and should be updated to new Energy Star units.



3. The attic hatch is quite drafty and can be sealed with a removable caulking such as Draft Attack.



4. Although you suspected the older windows were drafty, they were mostly OK &amp; sealed very well. They are triple pane windows that are still in good shape. Only 2 of them looked like they could be closed a bit tighter - all the rest closed &amp; sealed fine.



Savings: If you were able to reduce the overall air leakage by about 10%, this is estimated to save $ 152 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 4.7 GJ.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <ceilingsCathedral />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows />
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 182 litre electric hot water tank. In my previous heating section, I outline an opportunity for you to replace both the current 83% AFUE oil furnace and the electric hot water tank with a 95% AFUE "combi" propane boiler that will both heat your house and hot water. Please refer to that section of my report.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with a new 83% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE - note that Energy Star oil furnaces in Canada need to be a minimum 85% AFUE in order to qualify).



The main issue is the ability of this furnace (and the ductwork that serves the 4 levels of your house - including the secondary suite on the lower right side) to efficiently &amp; effectively maintain a comfortable temperature in all areas of the house. The furnace "operates" based upon the thermostat settings in the main living area (upper right section, above the secondary suite). When it is warm enough in that section of the house, it may be too cold on both lower levels and may be a bit too warm in the upper bedroom areas (as warm air will rise up the open staircase from the living room to the upper bedrooms.



If your house was being built today, I am pretty sure the builder would install a boiler, perhaps with in-floor heat and multiple zones - each one with its' own thermostat. I realize this would be near impossible to retrofit into your house now, however I think you should research the following with a few qualified heating contractors:



1. Install a 95% efficient condensing propane boiler



2. Have one zone from the boiler feed a heat exchange fancoil that can use the current furnace ductwork to feed all of the house, except the secondary suite (close off the heat vents in the ceiling of the secondary suite). You can use the current thermostat for this zone.



3. Floor level hot water rads can be installed in the secondary suite, and there is access through the crawlspace below to run a zone to &amp; connect these rads. All the heat runs through the crawlspace should be insulated. A new thermostat can be installed in the secondary suite for this zone.



4. This same boiler has the ability to supply your domestic hot water at ultra high efficiency and without ever running out. Because you have a secondary suite, you will either want to choose a boiler with a high continuous flow rate (litres per minute) or integrate some storage (possibly even the current electric hot water tank). The domestic hot water would have its' own zone from the boiler.



5. I'm not a huge fan of using the house boiler for heating areas outside the living space, however I'm sure you have seen many houses in Yellowknife, where they install a fancoil in the upper corner of the garage and run another zone from the boiler (with its' own thermostat in the garage) to provide temporary heat for when you are working on a vehicle or just want the temperature to be above zero (keep the snow &amp; ice off your vehicles).



If this type of system has an interest for you, I recommend an Energy Star qualified condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, and has the ability to operate in "combi" mode - also providing your domestic hot water.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas "combi" boiler, you could potentially save $ 3,282 per year in energy costs ($1,821 in heating savings &amp; $1,461 in hot water savings) , decrease your annual energy usage by 39.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. NOTE: the savings illustrated above are only for the design outlined in #1 through #4 above. Providing some heat to the garage would reduce the estimated savings &amp; I just want you to be aware of that.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has three toilets and the one in the main bathroom on the upper floor is an older unit that uses a lot of water &amp; it should be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing an older toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Flourescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (both kitchen range fans do not vent outside, however all 3 bathroom fans &amp; the clothes dryer do), indicated a pressure drop of 4.6 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not currently expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace vents up a chimney and IS susceptible to back drafting. This issue will be eliminated if you convert to a high efficiency, direct vent boiler.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following ventilation:

        - There is an HRV tucked away in the small void behind the kitchen (access panel behind the fridge)

              * This unit was not functioning at the time of my visit

        - 2 kitchen range hood fans that do not vent outside (one in the secondary suite) 

        - Three bathroom exhaust fans that do vent to the outside (one in the secondary suite)

        - A clothes dryer venting to the outside



NOTE 1: The HRV was not operating and likely has not been used for a very long time. The filters inside have likely not been cleaned recently. Based on the current air leakage of your house, it does not appear that the HRV is even required. If you decide to "decommission" this HRV, it may be wise to have the outside fresh air supply duct (to the HRV) redirected and connected to the return air plenum of the furnace (or new combi boiler fancoil) ductwork. This fresh air supply pipe should have a manual damper integrated so you can adjust the amount of outside fresh air entering your home:

        - open more if the indoor air feels a bit stale, stuffy &amp; condensation is building up on the windows

        - close more if the house feels dry (static electricity) &amp; there is no condensation building up on window



NOTE 2: There appears to be a ventilation system for the unheated crawlspace below the house. There is an exhaust fan that continuously blows air out the front of the house (ductwork comes up from the crawlspace through a secondary suite bedroom closet and then out the front wall). The air inlets to the crawlspace are in the far rear left corner of the foundation (2 or 3 small vents at sidewalk level, although the 2 on the back wall appear to be sealed and perhaps just the one by the left exit door is still open). I could feel suction at the vent entrance at sidewalk level near the left exit door. This ventilation system is good if there is moisture gathering in the crawlspace that needs to be vented out to prevent mold and poor air quality. However, if there is no moisture gathering in the crawlspace during the winter heating season, operating this fan during the cold winter months will make the floor above feel cool (even though that floor above is insulated).



You should consider replacing the bathroom exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR rated fan that use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels - and they have good internal dampers to reduce heat loss when they are not in use. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Yellowknife



The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil  - $1.21 per litre 

Propane - $0.68 per litre

Wood - $420.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



